chx:F1 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Background"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter describes the blockchain as an ordered, back-linked list of blocks in which every block commits to its parent through the previous-block hash of its header (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The description is confirmed by a real pair of blocks: block 277,315 has the parent hash of block 277,314, version 2, time 1388185038, nonce 4215469401 and merkle root 5e049f40..., the values of the book's listing, with 40 transactions in 43,560 bytes (Mempool Space, 2026). The block hash is the one-line double SHA-256 of the 80-byte header in the code (Bitcoin Core, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P64, chx:P16 .
chx:F2 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Background"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The header is 80 bytes and a block may be 4,000,000 bytes, so a block can be 50,000 times the size of its header, as the book says (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); the constant MAX_BLOCK_SERIALIZED_SIZE is 4,000,000 and the header class has the six fields in the order of the book's table (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Today the 971,538 headers of the main chain take about 78 megabytes at 80 bytes each, against a whole chain of 775,272 megabytes (Blockchain Charts, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P15, chx:P71 .
chx:F3 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Erratum for a reader who types the book's regtest session: the first command, createwallet with an empty name, is refused by Bitcoin Core 31.1 with the error 'Wallet name cannot be empty' (exit code 8), a fresh node has no wallet, and getnewaddress fails with 'No wallet is loaded'; the wallet needs a name (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Bitcoin Core developers, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P36 .
chx:F4 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Changed default: since release 28.0 the block files blk*.dat are scrambled with a random XOR key kept in blocks/xor.dat, and older releases and external software cannot read such a directory. In the run the first bytes of blk00000.dat were unreadable until the key d462ed9f917fe79d was applied, after which the file began with the magic fabfb5da and the size 285 of the genesis block. The book describes the blocks as plain data in the files (Bitcoin Core developers, 2024); (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P33, chx:P26, chx:P30, chx:P01 .
chx:F5 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The book's block table begins with a 4-byte block size, which is not part of a block as it travels or as consensus sees it but a field that Bitcoin Core writes in front of each block in its block files, after the magic bytes; on the test chain the genesis block took 293 bytes on disk, 285 plus 8 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Bitcoin Core, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P26 .
chx:F6 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Changed state: the book names testnet3 as the current testnet, but Bitcoin Core 28.0 added testnet4 as specified in BIP 94 (assigned 2024-05-27 to Fabian Jahr, now Deployed), and Bitcoin Core 31.1 still starts testnet3 with -testnet while its help text says that the support is deprecated and will be removed; Optech calls testnet4 a recently started replacement (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Jahr, 2024); (Bitcoin Core developers, 2024); (Bitcoin Core developers, 2026); (Bitcoin Optech, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P12, chx:P33, chx:P38, chx:P43 .
chx:F7 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Erratum of size: the book calls testnet3 a chain in excess of 30 GB, but Bitcoin Core 31.1 assumes 245 GB for it in chainparams.cpp, and on 2026-10-15 testnet3 stood at height 5,189,394, 6.3 times the number of blocks that one block in ten minutes since 2011-02-02 would have given and more than 5 times the 971,537 blocks of mainnet (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Mempool Space, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P20, chx:P70 .
chx:F8 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Erratum of detail: the book says that the minimum difficulty of testnet is half that of Bitcoin, but chainparams.cpp gives mainnet and testnet3 the same proof-of-work limit 00000000ffff..., so the minimum difficulty is 1 on both. The sentence agrees with the Bitcoin Wiki and not with the code. The second rule, that a block more than 20 minutes after its parent may use the minimum difficulty, is right (pow.cpp uses 2 x 10 minutes) and holds for testnet3, testnet4 and regtest (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P20, chx:P22, chx:P48 .
chx:F9 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Incomplete list: the book's tip says that test networks use addresses beginning with m, n or tb1, but the test chains also use the prefix 2 for script-hash addresses (base58 version 196) and regtest uses bcrt1 (bech32 prefix bcrt), as its own listing shows; the nodes of the run produced m, 2, tb1q and tb1p on testnet4 and signet, and bcrt1q and bcrt1p on regtest (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Bitcoin Core, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P20 .
chx:F10 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Changed value: the book's listing of the default signet shows 143,619 blocks and a size of 769,525,915 bytes and says that the default signet has about 150,000 blocks and is about a gigabyte; on 2026-10-15 its tip was at height 326,440, 2.27 times as many, and Bitcoin Core assumes 24 GB of disk space for it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Mempool Space, 2026); (Bitcoin Core, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P68, chx:P20 .
chx:F11 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The default signet is operated by two contributors, as the book says, and the reason is visible in its challenge: a 71-byte script that is a 1-of-2 multisig of two 33-byte keys, so that either operator can sign a block. BIP 325 was assigned on 2019-03-20 to Karl-Johan Alm and Anthony Towns, and Bitcoin Core 0.21.0 added the support with one public signet maintained (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Alm and Towns, 2019); (Bitcoin Core developers, 2021); (Bitcoin Optech, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P11, chx:P32, chx:P44 .
chx:F12 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The message-start bytes of a signet are not constants but the first four bytes of the double SHA-256 of the challenge script, so every custom signet has its own network magic: 0a03cf40 for the default signet, 54d26fbd for the challenge 51 and 7ec653a5 for the example of BIP 325, which lists the same value. The book does not mention this (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Alm and Towns, 2019); (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P20, chx:P11, chx:P01 .
chx:F13 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Erratum of arithmetic: the book says that the largest possible merkle proof is almost 10,000 times smaller than the largest block. The proof needs the 80-byte header and 448 bytes of path, 528 bytes, so a block of 4,000,000 bytes is 7,576 times larger; with a typical 250-byte transaction the ratio is 5,141, and 10,000 would need a proof of 400 bytes. The statement that a path has 10 to 14 hashes (320 to 448 bytes) is right (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01 .
chx:F14 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The design flaw in the merkle tree is real and has a history: the comment in merkle.cpp warns of CVE-2012-2459, Optech states that the vulnerability was reported in 2012 and fixed quietly, that a variation was reintroduced in 0.13.0 in 2016 and fixed in 0.14.0, and that another was found in 2023; on the test chain a block with a repeated last transaction had the same header and hash and was rejected as bad-txns-duplicate, after which the genuine block was accepted (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Bitcoin Optech, 2026); (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P17, chx:P42, chx:P01 .
chx:F15 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The book does not mention the second weakness of the tree: an inner node is the hash of 64 bytes, and a transaction of exactly 64 bytes without witness data can be mistaken for one, which Optech lists as CVE-2017-12842. BIP 54, the consensus cleanup, would make such transactions invalid, which fixes the weakness for light clients without a workaround (Bitcoin Optech, 2026); (Poinsot and Corallo, 2025); (Bitcoin Optech, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P42, chx:P14, chx:P45 .
chx:F16 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The weight limit is the real block limit: MAX_BLOCK_WEIGHT is 4,000,000 weight units and block 840,000 held 3,993,281 weight units in 2,325,617 bytes. Block templates of Bitcoin Core stop at 3,992,000 because 8,000 units are reserved (-blockreservedweight), and release 29.0 fixed a bug that had reserved the 4,000 units twice. The book says that transactions can total about 4,000,000 bytes (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Mempool Space, 2026); (Bitcoin Core developers, 2025); (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P18, chx:P66, chx:P34, chx:P01 .
chx:F17 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Changed output: the getblock listing of the book has no target and no coinbase_tx. Bitcoin Core 29.0 added the field target to getblock and getblockheader and 31.0 added the coinbase_tx object, and the genesis block of the test chain showed both (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Bitcoin Core developers, 2025); (Bitcoin Core developers, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P34, chx:P36 .
chx:F18 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The version field is no longer a version number: BIP 9 requires the top three bits 001 for blocks that signal, giving the range from 0x20000000, and the draft BIP 320 reserves sixteen bits (mask 0x1fffe000) for general use, with BIP 323 proposed as its replacement. Block 840,000 carries 0x2a5fe000 and the blocks of the test chain 0x20000000, while the book says only that the field's use has evolved (Wuille et al., 2015); (BtcDrak, 2018); (Mempool Space, 2026); (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P07, chx:P13, chx:P66, chx:P01 .
chx:F19 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Timewarp and testnet storms have shaped recent releases. BIP 94 for testnet4 starts the retarget from the first block of the previous period and bounds the time of the first block of a period to 600 seconds before its parent; Bitcoin Core 29.0 made block templates respect the timewarp rule on all networks and turned the rule off on regtest, and the consensus cleanup of BIP 54 would apply a timewarp fix to mainnet, which Optech's time warp topic explains (Jahr, 2024); (Bitcoin Core developers, 2025); (Poinsot and Corallo, 2025); (Bitcoin Optech, 2026); (Bitcoin Optech, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P12, chx:P34, chx:P14, chx:P37, chx:P45 .
chx:F20 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The genesis block holds a first reward that can never be spent, a quirk that the Bitcoin Wiki says may or may not have been intended, and a node on the test chain confirmed that getrawtransaction refuses the genesis coinbase, gettxout returns nothing and the UTXO set at height 0 is empty. Testnet4 has its own genesis coinbase (180 bytes, id 7aa0a7ae...) with the message 03/May/2024; the book does not mention either point (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026); (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Jahr, 2024); (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P46, chx:P20, chx:P12, chx:P01 .
chx:F21 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The timestamp is described by the book only as an approximate time. The rules are that it must be greater than the median of the previous 11 blocks (BIP 113) and not more than 2 hours ahead of the node's clock, and a 4-byte counter overflows on 2106-02-07; block 277,315 is 1,651 seconds later than its median time past (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Kerin and Friedenbach, 2015); (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026); (Wikipedia contributors, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P10, chx:P23, chx:P49, chx:P54 .
chx:F22 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Heights have moved: the book reports height 800,000 in mid 2023, and on 2026-10-15 the main chain was at height 971,537; block 840,000, mined on 2024-04-20 at 00:09:27 UTC, is the first with the reward of 3.125 coins. The book's number of 12,462.5 coins after 500 blocks on regtest is right, because the regtest reward halves every 150 blocks and not every 210,000 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Mempool Space, 2026); (Mempool Space, 2026); (Bitcoin Core, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P67, chx:P66, chx:P20 .
chx:F23 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Conclusion"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The ideas under the chapter are older than Bitcoin: Haber and Stornetta described hash-linked time-stamping in 1991, Ralph Merkle patented the hash tree in 1979 and described tree authentication in 1980, and Nakamoto's paper cites the first and uses the second for compressing old blocks (Haber and Stornetta, 1991); (Wikipedia contributors, 2026); (Merkle, 1980); (Nakamoto, 2008)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P40, chx:P52, chx:P41, chx:P39 .
chx:F24 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Conclusion"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The probability that an attacker with 10 percent of the hash rate catches up from 6 blocks behind is 0.0002428, from 1 block behind 0.2045873, and with 30 percent at 6 blocks 0.1321, computed again with the formula of the paper; this is the quantitative reason behind the book's picture of stable layers (Nakamoto, 2008); (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P39, chx:P01 .
chx:F25 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Conclusion"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Taken together, the chapter's account of the block, the chain and the merkle tree stands, and every structural claim could be recomputed. What is wrong or out of date lies at the edges: one arithmetic ratio, one sentence on testnet difficulty, the sizes and heights of 2023, the list of address prefixes, the testnet3 advice, the empty wallet name, and five changes of default or output since the book: XOR block files, testnet4, new getblock fields, signet magic by challenge and the reserved block weight (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Bitcoin Core developers, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P38 .
chx:Scorecard a sen0414:QualityScorecard ; rdfs:label "Quality scorecard"@en ; sen0414:topicCoverage "43 primary-source concepts inventoried from the textbook ontology." ; sen0414:citationStrength "73 sources, all opened and verified at 2026-10-15T00:00:00Z; 40 cited by findings." ; sen0414:methodologyCompliance "RDODI procedure v1.6.0 Stage 1." ; sen0414:reproducibility "Every source is a stable public URL; every claim names its sources." .
